  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Optimize the general dense-DP fallback. Use one in-place O(n) DP array, seed the origin correctly, and eliminate the row-copy pass. Since each addition is below 2*MOD, replace remainder with one conditional subtraction. Store obstacles in row-grouped, sorted, deduplicated lists and process obstacle-free spans without per-cell blockage tests; reset blocked positions to zero. Investigate buffered input and allocation overhead separately. Preserve n=1, blocked endpoints, repeated coordinates, and first-row/column behavior. Measure changes individually before combining them.",
        "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle algorithm. Precompute factorials and inverse factorials through 2n-2. For each distinct obstacle in row/column order, count paths reaching it while avoiding earlier obstacles: binomial paths from the origin minus contributions from earlier coordinate-wise preceding obstacles. Apply the same calculation to the destination. Use 64-bit modular products and normalize subtraction. Handle blocked endpoints explicitly. This gives approximately O(n + m log m + m²) work and an O(n) obstacle-free path. Retain a correct dense fallback; select sparse versus dense using measured work estimates from dimensions and obstacle count, never case identities.",
        "C": "Research four-core ARM64 execution of the dense recurrence. Develop a cache-tiled anti-diagonal wavefront: tiles depend on their top and left neighbors, while tiles on the same tile diagonal execute independently. Use per-worker rolling tile buffers and compact boundary frontiers rather than an O(n²) integer DP table. Ensure boundary publication and diagonal synchronization are race-free. Tune tile size and a serial cutoff through the unchanged measurement system. Do not naively parallelize dependent rows or columns. Stay within the existing build environment and retain a correct serial path when parallel overhead dominates.",
        "G": "Integrate measured programs, not presumed additive improvements. First retain the fastest validated standalone candidate; then compare selected combinations of A's kernel/input handling, B's sparse dispatch, and C's parallel kernel. Share obstacle normalization where useful, but include preprocessing and allocation costs in end-to-end timing. Use generic workload-based dispatch with 64-bit cost calculations. Revalidate every integrated candidate through the unchanged correctness system, including modular arithmetic, duplicate handling, tile boundaries, and endpoint initialization. Keep the standalone winner whenever integration regresses. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
        "analysis": "The relevant task is Problem D. The only measured program is the correct baseline: mean 0.079061 seconds across three tightly grouped samples, with four correctness cases. Their dimensions and obstacle distributions are unknown, so no bottleneck or optimized speedup is established. The source performs O(n²) DP, stores an n²-bit obstacle grid, copies each completed row, and uses remainder per cell. The independent research axes are scalar engineering, sparse combinatorial counting, and dependency-aware four-core parallelism. Every branch must preserve the exact modulo result for every valid input."
      },
